A||||17||8Ball||LED taillamp revamp||||1||
Z||000000||8Ball||11-04-2001||12:28 AM||||I have an Idea in the drawing process for a complete tail lamp revamp. But I have a question. I am going to be wiring Leds in a block (Brake, Turn, reverse lights, etc etc etc) Will the leds in each block need to be wired in serial?  Or should I make a circuit board for each? It would be a lot easier if just wiring in a series would work. <P>As for the project I am still hammering out some details. But in a couple months when I start on it I will keep everyone Informed.<P>Thanks,<BR>Randy W.||||reg||1
Z||000001||Nashco||11-04-2001||12:43 AM||||Doesn't voltage drop occur in series?  I'm thinking back to physics...voltage drops in series, remains constant in parallel?  Who remembers, or actually fiddles with electronics and knows <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> (I'm getting there, don't make fun of me...)<P>If this is true, and I think it is, the LEDs would lose voltage.  Eventually, you wouldn't have enough voltage to make a bright light at all.  Since they have to be wired in parallel, you would probably be better of making a circuit board than wiring them one at a time.<P>So, who can verify this?<P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000002||watts||11-04-2001||02:12 AM||||Remember that an LED is a diode.  They hit a specified brightness at a specified current, not voltage.  Usually 15ma's will give the rated intensity.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000003||8Ball||11-04-2001||02:25 AM||||Ok so would say 50 in series work?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||Ferrari_cdn||11-04-2001||02:42 AM||||But if one of them failed, all of them will go off and you would have to test every one of them to know which is the bad one.<P>------------------<BR><A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/ferrari_cdn" TARGET=_blank> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/ferrari_cdn.JPG">  </A>  <BR>AIM: CanadianFiero||||reg||1
Z||000005||Fierowrecker||11-04-2001||02:44 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by 8Ball:<BR><B>Ok so would say 50 in series work?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No...<BR>You need to know the working current of the diode, usually 30 milliamp...<BR>EACH diode needs to have a dropping resistor in series with the diode, and each resistor/diode combination needs to be in parallel...<BR>Asuming you are going to use 15 volts (maximum output of the alternator), you need a 500 ohm resistor in series (470 ohm standard will work), with each diode...<P>BTW, if you try to use ONE resistor that is a high wattage type, the LED's will not light with equal intensity...<BR>Now, basic ohms law will suggest a half watt resistor, in reality, a quarter watt will work and not heat stress...<P>If you are going to use perf board to mount the LED's on, the resistors could be mounted on the back side, alowing the LED's maximum room...<BR>To ruggedize the assembly, use a dab of RTV under the LED as you mount it, and also on the resistors after soldering...<P>If I understand what you are trying to do, it should look very good!<BR>crash...<p>[This message has been edited by Fierowrecker (edited 11-04-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||Nashco||11-04-2001||03:51 AM||||HAHA..my answer SUCKED..lol.  Good luck with that, I am obviously not ready to step up to the electronics plate <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>Bryce<BR>88 GT||||reg||1
Z||000007||MrFieroUK||11-04-2001||09:39 AM||||Correct when you say each LED has a specific optimum current at which full brightness is achieved. However, that corresponds to a slight change in voltage drop across the diode which also has to be taken into consideration. <BR>Typically a red LED will have a forward voltage drop of approx. 2.2 volts. If the required current is optimally 20 to 25 mA the series resistor value (in ohms) will need to be calculated on the supply voltage LESS the forward diode voltage i.e.<P>vsupply = 13.8volts nominal.<BR>vf diode = 2.2 volts.<BR>therefore voltage value for calculations = 13.8-2.2 = 11.6<P>Now using Ohms law (resistance = voltage divided by current) we get R (resistance) = 11.8 /0.025 = 472 ohms. Nearest value (preferred value range) is the common 470 ohm resistor. You also need to take into account the power the resistor will be disipating (ohms law W (watts or power) = (V)olts x (A)mps) Therefore 11.8 x 0.025 = 0.295 Watts. The nearest prefferred value type is 1/2 Watt. So each resistor will be a 470 ohm 1/2 watt type. Use a carbon film type (cheapest and robust, typically 5% value tolerance).<P>Have you considered the total power consumption? Its low at about 17 Watts for 50 LEDs but could be a lot lower and use a lot less resistors.<P>Because each red LED has a forward voltage drop of around 2.2V (Green & yellow are different, blue and white are WAY different.) you could put several in series with one current limiting resistor, and then put a string of this configuration in parallel, thereby dramatically reducing current consumption and component count.<P>Lets consider a couple of factors here. First LED brightness can vary from not on to full brightness over a matter of 200 millivolts (0.2V) therefore it doesn't take much fluctuation in supply voltage to make a big difference in taillamp brightness. Just the tolerance of output on a regulator can make a big difference. Anything from 13.5 to 14.3 volts is not unusual and will change as the regulator components age from use and heat. Some alts. put out as much as 15 volts if thaey were designed to be used with gel type electrolyte batteries.<P>What this means is you don't want to use all your "headroom" up by stringing say 6 LEDS in a series row (2.2V x 6 = 13.2V leaving only 0.3 to 1.1V headroom - no resistor exists to safely limit current in a 400% tolerance range!! For example the current of 0.025A at VS=13.5V is 12 ohms, but at 14.3V, a 12 ohm resistor would be passing over 1/2 Amp. Result? lotsa smoke and dead components.)<P>Given the above, and the fact I've already done this on several cars, the best compromise is 3 LEDs in a row with a current limit resistor of 270 ohms will give about 0.025A at a nominal VS of 13.8 volts. Do the maths and you will find the tolerance change for VS is well catered for while reducing the current consumed and numbers of resistors by two-thirds, a significant saving in parts, time and juice.<P>The other important thing to consider is brightness and angle of visibility. Not all LEDS are created equal. For this application you will need Super High Bright LEDs. these give off incredible amounts of light for the same amount of current. The best around st the moment are Hewlett Packards HMPL series with ouputs around 6300 millicandles and a 30 degree cone of visibility. You can get brighter but the degree of visibility will go right down to 5 degrees - useless for this application. the cone of visibility is that which gives maximum brightness as measured from the centre (face on) view of the LED out to the edge of the cone. After that the visibility (apparent brightness)  drops off dramatically. THIS MEANS you will need to consider physical orientation of the LEDS VERY CAREFULLY. FMVSS lighting requirements typically demand rear brake/turn/position lamps to have an angle of visibility of +/- 45 degrees horizontally, +/- 25 degrees vartically, from the centre of the lamp (NOTE FMVSS requirements in force for this vehicle also specify the minimum light output of any given lamp). So, depending on your lamp cluster, you MAY have to consider offsetting the centre line of some LEDS to the left and right by say 30 degrees or so.<P>ALSO if you change out turn signal lamps for LEDs you will need to change your flasher can, because the loading will be reduced by close to the value of one lamp. The turn signal LEDs will either stay on or not come on at all. A Wagner 532 Heavy Duty flasher should be substituted in for both the turn signal and emergency (hazzard) flashers (couple bucks ea?). They're available from just about all US auto parts stores.<P>Good luck!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||3
Z||000008||8Ball||11-04-2001||12:29 PM||||hmmm, Ok this is going to take a few readings to soak in  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Thanks everyone. I will try to do up a photoshop rendering of what I am going to attempt. I just have to find a good straight on picture on notchback tail lights. Needs to be rather close as well.<P><BR>Thanks for all the info guys.||||reg||1
Z||000009||8Ball||11-04-2001||01:44 PM||||Ok I have come upon a realization. I stink at image editing.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> This is basically what I was going to do. The turn signals would be on the very end and would be arrows.<BR>The brake lights would be right next to those and would be the Fiero emblem.<BR>And the backup lights would be the word Pontiac in the middle in white. Everything else in red. And every bit leds. Can anyone with some photo editing skills do that for me? Please  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> Then I can show you guys what it is I am trying to do.||||reg||1
Z||000010||theogre||11-04-2001||02:04 PM||||Find my old mirror turn signal project that I had to abandon when I got sick.... There's allot of relevant info there including calculation of resistor values. It may be in the tec archives...<P>Fist off..... The LEDs you see in auomotive lamping applications usually aren't wired in straight series. When they are in series the number in each group is kept small, like 3-4 per group.<P>They are often wired in a lattic (or ladder) network such that a number of LEDs can fail and the rest will still work. Propperly configured latticies can suffer 50% or more failure and still light.<P>Second, You Don't need a resistor for each LED. The ressistor choice however is a little complicated. The correct resistor for a lattic network varies some depending on the method of wiring and size of lattic. <P>Third, resistor choice will varry depending on exactly what LEDs are used. You need to check the specs for whatever kind you get. Obviously, diferant colors usually need differant values but even leds of the same color have some variation.<P>You definatly want to use LED's with a wide viewing angle. 30degrees should be the minimum.<P>For something as critical as tail lights... There's are links in the safety article in my cave you might want to read over. You'll want to visit the Index of Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standards section 108. There's also a web site linked that is covering various issues with aftermarket lighting, including a number of recalls. <P>Those articles may be dificult to understand for some readers but they will help you understand how to build your lights so they will do the job they need to. I.E. hopefully keep you from getting ass ended.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000011||MrFieroUK||11-04-2001||03:16 PM||||You say you want to use white LEDs for the backup section? Hope your pockets are deep! these babies are relatively new in the electronics market place - have low light output compared with the super bright reds - a forward voltage drop of around 4 to 4.5 volts and cost... are you ready for this? around 5 to 10 times more than red LEDs.<P>Also they are only available from a very few manufacturers currently, the most prominent being Multicomp. You're best sticking with filament bulbs for your backup lamps.||||reg||1
Z||000012||Banzai||11-04-2001||04:44 PM||||I'm not sure if this will help. I recieved a Digi-Key catalog a while back and it seems to me that they carried LEDs with built in resistors. I'm not sure of the viewable angle etc. but check them out at Digikey.com.<P>Banzai !!!!!!!!||||reg||1
Z||000013||MinnGreenGT||11-05-2001||10:30 AM||||Instead of reengineering the whole thing, why not get LED bulb-replacements- just mask off the symbols you want to use?  Like my Clear GT Taillights... <A HREF="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/011212.html" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fiero.nl/forum/Forum1/HTML/011212.html</A>  just instead of using a light lens, just mask off the shapes. It'd be pretty easy really (and the LED bulbs are now only $10ea at JCWhitney and you can get colored 194 bulbs for the PONTIAC just about anywhere. Oh yeah, when you replace your standard bulbs with the LEDs, you'll need a new flasher relay.  A variable-load relay ($12 for a heavy duty one at my local parts shop). Good luck.<P>------------------<BR>              <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/GreenGT6.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000014||8Ball||11-05-2001||04:09 PM||||Hmmm ya know minn that might not be a bad idea  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000015||Mark||11-05-2001||11:12 PM||||8ball,<P>Getting through the electrical design may be the easiest part of this project!  As discussed by others, there are significant safety issues associated with modifying lighting.  Angle of visibility is certainly a big one.  Also, what about reflective lenses?  I don't know what the state inspection laws are in TN, but in PA, I doubt you would pass safety inspaection with ANY modifications.  Without reflective surfaces, you probably wouldn't last a day before being pulled over.||||reg||1
Z||000016||MinnGreenGT||11-06-2001||11:14 AM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mark:<BR><B>8ball,<P>Getting through the electrical design may be the easiest part of this project!  As discussed by others, there are significant safety issues associated with modifying lighting.  Angle of visibility is certainly a big one.  Also, what about reflective lenses?  I don't know what the state inspection laws are in TN, but in PA, I doubt you would pass safety inspaection with ANY modifications.  Without reflective surfaces, you probably wouldn't last a day before being pulled over.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><BR>Wrong! (Thankfully) The Fiero does not use the taillights as a reflective area- there are reflectors built into the lower bumpers on all Fieros (right below the taillights). And there is reflective material on both left and right sides of the car (rear "marker" lights double as reflectors).<BR>So the issue is invalid.  As to viewing angle- my LEDs are brighter and more visible than any standard bulb.<P>BTW: I had a cop <B>right</B> behind me the day after I installed my Clear Taillights.  He was directly behind me for more than 10 minutes (and we turned numerous times) and he didn't check me twice for the turn signals/LEDs.<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/reflectors.jpg">  <BR>Notice the reflection from the lower bumper and right side?  That't the stuff.  Don't worry about it!<p>[This message has been edited by MinnGreenGT (edited 11-06-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000017||Mark||11-06-2001||06:11 PM||||I stand corrected.||||reg||1
